WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.645
An AI agent is an application in which a model can choose

00:00:03.645 --> 00:00:06.986
a next action, inspect its result, and continue toward a goal.

00:00:07.136 --> 00:00:10.620
The model proposes; Java code controls execution.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.902
A chatbot can answer text without taking any action.

00:00:14.052 --> 00:00:17.358
A workflow follows paths selected by application code.

00:00:17.508 --> 00:00:20.314
An agent may select among those paths at runtime.

00:00:20.464 --> 00:00:23.876
These are degrees of autonomy, not levels of intelligence.

00:00:24.026 --> 00:00:27.523
Start with a narrow user goal and a measurable success condition.

00:00:27.673 --> 00:00:32.878
For order support, success means identifying the correct customer’s order, reporting its

00:00:32.878 --> 00:00:36.348
actual status, and explaining the permitted next step.

00:00:36.498 --> 00:00:40.349
A fluent answer is insufficient if the order belongs to another customer.

00:00:40.499 --> 00:00:44.341
Choose deterministic code whenever rules already determine the right action.

00:00:44.491 --> 00:00:48.458
Add a model where language understanding or flexible selection is useful.

00:00:48.608 --> 00:00:53.249
Our support assistant has two read-only tools: lookupOrder and searchPolicy.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:58.527
A scripted model first requests order A100, then the policy, then produces

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:00.664
an answer with source identifiers.

00:01:00.814 --> 00:01:03.996
The same engine can use a local language model through Ollama.

00:01:04.146 --> 00:01:09.142
Scripted mode demonstrates orchestration and is repeatable; it does not perform AI

00:01:09.142 --> 00:01:09.559
inference.

00:01:09.709 --> 00:01:13.405
No tool pays money, modifies an order, or runs a shell.

00:01:13.555 --> 00:01:15.037
Run Main in scripted mode.

00:01:15.187 --> 00:01:19.440
Draw the request path and identify every place the application can reject

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:20.149
a decision.

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:24.014
Compare an agent with a simple if statement for checking order status.

00:01:24.164 --> 00:01:30.294
The application validates input, action type, tool name, arguments, trusted identity, output

00:01:30.294 --> 00:01:33.869
size, number of calls, and elapsed time.

00:01:34.019 --> 00:01:37.663
For a known order ID and a single status lookup, direct code

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:38.270
is simpler.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.897
An agent helps when the user describes a problem requiring several information

00:01:42.897 --> 00:01:43.270
sources.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:44.091
Question.

00:01:44.241 --> 00:01:45.730
When would you avoid an agent?

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:46.456
Answer.

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:51.246
When a deterministic rule or fixed workflow satisfies the requirement more cheaply

00:01:51.246 --> 00:01:52.019
and predictably.

00:01:52.169 --> 00:01:52.840
Question.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.576
Who executes a tool call?

00:01:54.726 --> 00:01:55.303
Answer.

00:01:55.453 --> 00:01:56.543
The application.

00:01:56.693 --> 00:02:00.912
Model output is a request for execution, not an authorization grant.
